== A Husband’s Kind Conduct toward His Wife

=== §. 40. #emph[Of a husband’s amiable countenance toward his wife.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 3, §. 10.]

A husband’s conduct toward his wife must correspond #footnote[A husband’s conduct must be mild.] to his speech, or else all its mildness will seem mere compliment.

A man’s conduct includes his #emph[countenance, gesture, actions;] and mildness must be seated in all of them.

1\. His countenance in his wife’s presence, and toward his #footnote[1\. A mild countenance.] wife, must be composed to an amiable pleasantness. His authority over her and his eminence above her must not make him forget the close bond and union between them.

Under the face and countenance I include the head, brow, eyes, lips and such other parts as are, according to how they are set, signs of amiability or of discontent. Now among and above the other parts of the body, the outward composition of the countenance soonest and best declares the inward disposition of the heart. By Esau’s pleasant countenance Jacob perceived that Esau was reconciled to him in his heart, and so he said, “I have seen your face, and it is like #footnote[Gen 33:10.] seeing the face of God,” that is, an amiable, gracious countenance. On this ground David asked God “to shine the light of His #footnote[Ps 4:6.] countenance upon him,” so that by it he might know the favor and love of God toward him. On the other side, by a frowning and scowling face, by hanging down the head, pouting the lips and the like, anger, malice, grief and other such feelings of the heart are shown. By Cain’s downcast #footnote[Gen 4:6.] countenance God discerned that anger and envy were in his heart; by Laban’s countenance#footnote[Gen 31:2.] Jacob observed that Laban’s affection had turned from him. A wife, then, seeing mildness and amiability in her husband’s face, sees it as the face of God, and in it, as in a looking glass, sees the kindness and love of his heart. And so her heart is all the more firmly knit to him by it, and she is moved all the more to respect him.

=== §. 41. #emph[Of husbands’ too great austerity.]

Contrary to this is 1. A lofty, proud countenance, like that of an imperious #footnote[Contrary. 1. A lofty brow.] lord over his vassals.

2\. A grim, stern countenance, like that of a judge over poor prisoners. #footnote[2\. A grim look.]

3\. A scowling, frowning countenance, like that of a discontented #footnote[3\. A frowning forehead.] creditor over a desperate debtor.

4\. A fierce, fiery countenance, like that of an angry king over a #footnote[4\. A fiery eye.] subject who has displeased him.

As these and similar countenances show a proud, stubborn, furious, discontented disposition of heart, so they cannot but give a wife great discontent, indeed greatly frighten her, since she is only a weak vessel, and turn her heart and love away from him.

=== §. 42. #emph[Of a husband’s affectionate gesture with his wife.]

#smallcaps[II]. A husband’s gesture ought to be so affectionate and amiable #footnote[2\. Mild gesture.] toward his wife that others may see that he is her husband, and his wife may be moved to be affectionate toward him. Those who are ready to show themselves kind and mild husbands in this way are prone to go too far, and so to fall into an extreme on the right hand. For some are never content except when they have their wives in their laps, always hugging, kissing and fondling them, no matter in what company. In this they show more flightiness, foolishness and doting than true kindness and love, which does not forget a husbandly gravity, sobriety, modesty and decency.

Some do not hesitate to cite Isaac’s caressing of Rebekah#footnote[Gen 26:8.] to #footnote[Objection.] justify their lasciviousness.

But they forget that what Isaac did was done when he and his #footnote[Answer.] wife were alone: he was seen through a window. Much greater liberty is granted to man and wife when they are alone than in company. Besides, there are many other ways to show kindness and familiarity than by flightiness and wantonness.

=== §. 43. #emph[Of a husband’s aloofness toward his wife.]

Contrary to the familiarity I speak of is (as we say) #emph[aloofness,] when a husband carries himself toward his wife as if she were a stranger to him. If he comes into company where his wife is, of all the women there he will not turn to her or take notice of her. This fault is all the greater if such a man is of a free, pleasant manner, and is accustomed to be merry and friendly with other women. Though his mirth and familiarity are such as are not unbecoming to a Christian, yet since his manner toward his wife is of another kind, it may be a means of breeding jealousy in her. Many think that outward kind gestures toward a wife are foolish doting; but if they knew what a means it is to stir up, increase and preserve love in a wife’s heart toward her husband, they would think otherwise.

=== §. 43. #emph[Of a husband’s giving favors to his wife.]

#smallcaps[III]. Actions are, of all things, the most real demonstrations of true kindness, and a husband must not fail in them if he would have his kind speech, countenance and gesture taken in good part. Kindness and mildness in action consist in giving #emph[favors] (as we say) to his wife. This is expressly noted of Elkanah, who every year gave #emph[favors] to #footnote[1 Sam 1:4, 5.] his wives. As a husband shows his love to his wife by this, so he will greatly move her to do every duty to him. A small gift, given freely as an act of kindness, not as a debt but in testimony of love, works more on the heart of her to whom it is given than much more given by contract, or for a work done, so that it may seem to be earned.

In giving favors to a wife, a husband ought to be more bountiful and generous than to others, so that she may see by it that he loves her above all; as it is noted that Elkanah gave “Hannah a double portion, for he loved her.”#footnote[1 Sam 1:4.] And in giving favors it is best to give them with his own hands, unless he is away from her.

=== §. 44. #emph[Of husbands beating their wives.]

Contrary to this are the furious and spiteful actions of many unkind husbands #emph[(heads too heady),] whose favors are cuffs, #footnote[#emph[Vipera virus ob venerationem nuptiarum euomit. Et tu duritiem animi, tu feritatem, tu crudelitatem ob vnionis reuerentiam non deponis? Basil. Hexaem. hom.] 7. #emph[Ambr. Hexaem. l.] 5. #emph[c.] 7. #emph[vrget hanc ipsam similitudinem.] \[presses this very comparison.\]] blows, strokes and lashes. In this they are worse than the venomous viper. For the viper casts out its poison for its mate’s sake; and will you not, O husband, out of regard for that close union between you and your wife, lay aside your fierceness and cruelty? Many wives, because of their husbands’ fury, are worse off than servants: for

1\. Those who will not give a servant a blow do not care what load they lay on their wives.

2\. Whereas servants are under the tyranny of such furious men only for a set time and term, poor wives are tied to them all their life long.

3\. Wives cannot have as good a remedy by the help of the law against cruel husbands as servants may have against cruel masters.

4\. Masters do not have such opportunity to exercise their cruelty on servants as husbands have on wives, who are to be continually at table and in bed with their husbands.

5\. The nearer wives are to their husbands, and the dearer they ought to be to them, the more grievous blows must be when they are given by a husband’s hand,#footnote[#emph[Huiusmodi virum (siquidem vir appellandus est potius quàm fera) & patricidae, & matricidae similem dixeris. Chrys. hom.] 26. #emph[in] 1 Cor 11.] than by a master’s.

6\. The less power and authority a husband has to strike his wife than a master has to strike a servant, the heavier his blows seem, and the worse a wife’s case seems in that respect than a servant’s. Not unfitly, therefore, is such a man (if he may be thought a man rather than a beast) said to be like a father-killer and a mother-killer.

#emph[Question.] Is a husband, then, not allowed to beat his wife? #footnote[A husband must not beat his wife.]

#emph[Answer.] With submission to better judgments, I think he must not. My reasons are these.

1\. There is no warrant anywhere in Scripture #footnote[#emph[Reasons.] 1. No warrant in the word.] for it, by precept or example. Though this argument is negative, yet for the point in hand it is a forcible argument in two respects. 1. Because Scripture has so plentifully and particularly set out the distinct duties of husbands and wives, and yet has said nothing about a husband’s striking and beating his wife. 2. Because it has also spoken plentifully and particularly of all who are to correct others, of their manner of correcting, of the bearing of correction by those who are to be corrected, and of the use they are to make of it; and yet it says nothing at all about a husband’s punishing, or a wife’s bearing punishment, in this way. Since Scripture is so silent on this point, we may well infer that God has not ranked wives among those in the family who are to be corrected.

2\. That small disparity which (as I have #footnote[§. 4.] shown before) is #footnote[2\. Small disparity.] between man and wife does not permit so high a power in a husband, and so low a servitude in a wife, that he should beat her. Can it be thought reasonable that she who is the man’s lifelong bedfellow, who has power over his body, who #footnote[Summa iniuria est vitae sociam, & in rebus necessarijs tibi coniunctam, tanquam mancipium ignominia afficere. \[It is the greatest injury to disgrace, as if she were a slave, the companion of your life, joined to you in the necessary things of life.\] Chrys. ibid.] is a joint parent of the children and a joint governor of the family, should be beaten by his hands? What if children or servants should know of it? (as they surely will: for how can such a thing be done in the house without those of the house knowing it?) Can they respect her as a mother or a mistress, when she is under correction as well as they?

3\. The close bond, and the very union, that is between #footnote[3\. Close union.] man and wife does not allow such dealing to pass between them. The wife is as a man’s self: “The two will be one flesh.”#footnote[Eph 5:31.] No man but a frenzied, furious, desperate wretch will beat himself. Two sorts of men are noted in Scripture as cutting and gashing their own flesh: #emph[idolaters,] like the #footnote[1 Kgs 18:28.] prophets of Baal, and #emph[demoniacs,] like #footnote[Mark 5:5.] the man who was possessed with a legion of devils. Such are those who beat their wives: either blinded in their understanding, or possessed with a devil.

#emph[Objection.] He who is soundest in his wits will allow his body to be pinched, pricked, lanced and otherwise pained, if it is needful and beneficial.

#emph[Answer.] 1. A man’s heart will not allow him to do any of these things himself. There are surgeons whose work it is to do such things, and if the surgeon himself needs any such remedy for his own body, he will use the help of another surgeon. If the case is such that a wife must be beaten, it is more fitting for a husband to refer the matter to a public magistrate (who is like an approved and licensed surgeon) and not to do it with his own hands.

2\. Though some parts of the body may be treated so, not every part may, such as the #emph[heart,] which the wife is to the man.

3\. The comparison does not hold. For the pinching, lancing, #emph[etc.] named above is no punishment for any fault, as the beating of a wife in question is. There is no question that a man who has the skill may, if need be, open a vein, lance a boil, splint a broken bone or set a dislocated joint in his wife’s body, which may be more painful than correction; and in this the comparison holds, but not in the other.

2\. #emph[Objection.] There is as close a union between Christ and his church as between man and wife; yet Christ does not refrain from correcting and punishing his church.

#emph[Answer.] There is a twofold relation between Christ and the church. He is a husband to it, having made it “of His flesh, and of His bones,” and he is a supreme Lord over it, having “all authority #footnote[Eph 5:30. Matt 28:18.] in heaven and on earth” given to him. He punishes in this latter respect, not in the former. A husband is not such a supreme lord over his wife; therefore Christ’s example is no warrant for him.

4\. There is no hope of any good coming from a husband’s #footnote[4\. No profit: much injury.] beating of his wife. For where the one who is corrected is persuaded that the one who corrects has no authority or right to do so, that one will not be brought to take it patiently, but will resist and strive, if possible, to get the mastery. Let a stranger strike a grown child or a servant who will patiently bear many blows at a parent’s or master’s hand, and they will turn on that stranger and try to give him as good as he brings. Now since a wife has no ground to be persuaded that her husband has authority to #footnote[#emph[Adhibitis verberibus morbus non tollitur, sed augetur, & exasperatur. Melch. Iun. quaest. Polit. par.] 2.] beat her, what hope is there that she will patiently bear it and be bettered by it? Or rather, is it not likely that she will, if she can, rise against him, get the mastery over him (as many do), and never do any duty rightly? A fault in a wife is not taken away but increased by blows.

#emph[Objection.] Smart and pain may make her dread her husband, stand in awe of him, and do her duty the better.

#emph[Answer.] Such dread and awe befit neither a husband’s place, to demand them, nor a wife’s place, to give them. Though she may be brought by force to yield some outward subjection, yet inward hatred of her husband’s person may be joined with it, which is as bad as outward disobedience, if not worse.

#emph[Objection.] She may be of so outrageous a disposition that she will not be kept within any bounds except by #footnote[#emph[Nullum sit tam magnum peccatum, quod ad verberandum vxorem compellat. Chrys. hom.] 26. #emph[in] 1 Cor 11.] force.

1\. #emph[Answer.] It was answered long ago that “no fault should be so great as to compel a husband to beat his wife.”

2\. #emph[Answer.] Other forcible means may be used besides beating by her husband’s hands. She may be restrained in her liberty, denied the things she most desires, and kept shut up, as it were, under guard; and, if no other means will serve, she may be handed over to the magistrate. Then, if she is of so servile a disposition that she will be kept under by no other means than by fear and force, by smart and pain, she may fear the magistrate and feel his hand rather than her husband’s.

#emph[Objection.] If a wife grows so mannish, or rather mad, as to try to strike and beat her husband, may he not beat her in that case to make her stop her outrage?

#emph[Answer.] I do not doubt that the good provision the law makes to preserve a man’s life may be applied to this purpose. The law absolutely condemns all murder; yet if a man is so assaulted that there is no way to preserve his own life but by taking the life of the one who assaults him, it does not condemn him as a murderer, because he did it #emph[in defense of himself.] So if a husband #footnote[Se defendendo.] is set on by his wife, it is lawful and advisable that he defend himself; and if he can do it no other way but by striking her, that is not to be counted an unlawful beating of her.

=== §. 45. #emph[Of a husband’s bearing with his wife’s infirmities.]

So much for the husband’s avoiding of offense; now a word about his bearing with offense.

It is a general duty, common to all people of every sort, to “bear one #footnote[Gal 6:2.] another’s burdens.” In this wide sense even a wife is to bear her husband’s burden, because he, like everyone else, is liable to slip and fall, and so needs to be supported. Yet this duty belongs to a husband in a more special and distinctive way, and that in two respects.

1\. Of the two, he is more bound than his wife, because in relation to his wife he is the #emph[stronger:] for she is “the weaker vessel,” #footnote[#emph[Considera mulierem debile vas esse: tu autem vir propterea princeps factus es, vt subditae feras imbecillitatem.] \[Consider that the woman is a weak vessel; and you, the man, were made ruler for this reason, that you might bear the weakness of her who is subject to you.\] #emph[Chrys. hom.] 26. #emph[in] 1 Cor 11.] 1 Pet 3:7. But the #emph[strong] are most bound “to bear with the infirmities of the weak,” Rom 15:1.

2\. He is bound to bear with his wife more than with anyone else, because of the close union between them: he who cannot bear with his wife, #emph[his flesh,] can bear with nobody. The reason the apostle gives to move a man to dwell with his wife according to knowledge, and to give honor to her, implied in this phrase, “as to the weaker vessel,”#footnote[1 Pet 3:7.] shows that this is a duty belonging distinctively to a husband, in and by which he may both show his knowledge and wisdom and also do honor to his wife. For why is he reminded of her weakness, but to show that he should bear with her?

As that phrase implies the duty, so it also implies a good reason to enforce it. For precious things that we value highly are handled the more tenderly and carefully the weaker they are, such as china dishes and crystal glasses; and of all parts of the body, the eye is handled most tenderly. Now what things, what persons, are more dear and precious than a wife? Yet she is also a weak vessel; therefore she is much to be borne with.

For a husband’s better direction in this, a distinction must be #footnote[Directions to husbands for bearing with their wives’ infirmities.] made between infirmities: some are #emph[natural imperfections,] others are #emph[actual transgressions.] Natural imperfections are #emph[inward] (such as slowness of mind, dullness of understanding, shortness of memory, quickness of temper, etc.) or outward (such as lameness, blindness, deafness, or any other defect and deformity of body). These infirmities should breed pity, compassion, sympathy, yes, and greater tenderness and respect, but no offense. Note Abraham’s example in this case: his wife was barren, yet he did not despise #footnote[Neque Saram tanquam sterilem contempsit Abraham: neque quicquam tale exprobauit. Si vxor pauper sit, noli exprobare: & si stulta, noli ei insultare, \[If your wife is poor, do not reproach her; and if she is foolish, do not insult her,\] Ibid. Vxor iracunda etc. admonenda & adiuvanda consilio, & omni conatu annitendum, vt illis liberetur affectibus, \[An angry wife, etc., is to be admonished and helped with counsel, and every effort is to be made that she may be freed from those passions,\] Ibid.] her for it, nor reproach her with any such thing.

Actual transgressions are breaches of God’s law. Those meant here are such as tend most directly to his own disquiet and disadvantage, such as shrewishness, waywardness, fussiness, stubbornness, etc. In bearing with these a husband must especially show his wisdom, and that in various ways.

1\. By using the best and mildest means he can to remedy them, such as meek admonition, timely advice, gentle entreaty and compassionate affection. Elkanah, thinking that his wife was at fault in her distress, dealt with her in this way and supported her.

2\. By removing the stone she stumbles over, taking away the occasion (as far as he fittingly can) that makes her offend. So Abram, and that by God’s direction, put Hagar and her son out of the house, because they were an offense to Sarah.

3\. By turning his eyes away (if the matter is not great, but #footnote[1 Sam 1:8. Gen 21:14.] such as may be tolerated) and taking no notice of the offense, but rather passing it by, as if he did not perceive it. Solomon says that “it is to a man’s glory to overlook an offense,”#footnote[Prov 19:11. Eccl 7:23.] and he urges a man “not to pay attention to every word that is spoken.”

4\. By forgiving and forgetting it (if notice has been taken of it). Jacob took notice of Rachel’s rash and perverse demand, for he #footnote[Gen 30:1, 2, #emph[etc.]] rebuked her for it; yet since he readily yielded to what she afterward urged on him, it appears that he forgave the offense, if he did not forget it.

The best test of a man’s love for his wife, and of his wisdom #footnote[Reason. The best test of a man’s wisdom and love.] in ordering it, lies in this point of bearing with offenses. Not to be offended with a wife who gives no offense is not praiseworthy: heathen men may go that far. Note what Christ says of this case: “If you#super[+] love those who love you#super[+], and do good to #footnote[Matt 5:46. Luke 6:32, 33.] those who do good to you#super[+], what credit and reward is that to you#super[+]? For tax collectors and sinners do the same.” But gently to forbear, and wisely to pass over offenses when they are given, and not to be provoked when cause of provocation is given, is a true Christian virtue, a virtue that becomes husbands better than any other kind of men.

=== §. 46. #emph[Of husbands’ testiness.]

Contrary to this is testiness and irritability, when husbands are moved by the least provocation, like tinder catching fire at the least spark that falls on it. Indeed, many are like gunpowder, which not only catches fire but also breaks out into a violent flame at the least touch of fire. As gunpowder is dangerous to keep in a house, so such husbands are dangerous to be joined as closely to wives as marriage joins them. If it is said that, as gunpowder does no harm if fire does not come near it, so they are good and kind if they are not provoked and displeased, I answer that we have a proverb that says, “The devil is good while he is pleased,” yet it is not safe to have the devil too near. It is as impossible (considering man’s weakness) for a man to live and keep company with anyone and not give offense, as for flint stones to beat and dash against one another for long and no spark of fire to come from them. How then can it be thought possible for a wife, who is so continually in her husband’s company, and is the weaker vessel, to live without giving him offense? It is not a very kind saying that husbands use, especially when they are told of their unkindness: “Let my wife deserve favor, and she will have it.” How little favor would such husbands have from Christ their husband, if he were of that mind toward them!

Thus far the first part of a husband’s good management of his authority has been handled: a tender respect for his wife.

The second is a provident care for her.
